      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 3, 4, 6, and 7. The LCM of these numbers will give us the number of school days from today when all four tutors will be working together again in the math lab.\n\nLet's start by finding the LCM of 3, 4, 6, and 7.\n\n1. The prime factorization of each number is:\n   - \\(3 = 3\\)\n   - \\(4 = 2^2\\)\n   - \\(6 = 2 \\times 3\\)\n   - \\(7 = 7\\)\n\n2. The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:\n   - The highest power of 2 is \\(2^2\\).\n   - The highest power of 3 is \\(3\\).\n   - The highest power of 7 is \\(7\\).\n\nSo, the LCM is:\n\\[\nLCM = 2^2 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 4 \\times 3 \\times 7 = 84\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the next time all four tutors will be working together in the math lab is in \\(\\boxed{84}\\) school days.",
