      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Reasoning and answer:\n\nGiven:\n- Darren works every third school day.\n- Wanda works every fourth school day.\n- Beatrice works every sixth school day.\n- Chi works every seventh school day.\n\nTo determine when all four tutors will next be working together, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their working intervals.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Find the LCM of the intervals**:\n   - LCM of 3, 4, 6, and 7.\n\n2. **Prime Factorization**:\n   - \\(3 = 3\\)\n   - \\(4 = 2^2\\)\n   - \\(6 = 2 \\times 3\\)\n   - \\(7 = 7\\)\n\n3. **Determine the LCM**:\n   - The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorization.\n   - Highest power of 2: \\(2^2\\)\n   - Highest power of 3: \\(3\\)\n   - Highest power of 7: \\(7\\)\n\n   Therefore, LCM = \\(2^2 \\times 3 \\times 7\\).\n\n4. **Calculate the LCM**:\n   \\[\n   2^2 = 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4 \\times 3 = 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   12 \\times 7 = 84\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, the least common multiple of 3, 4, 6, and 7 is 84.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - All four tutors will next be working together in 84 school days.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{84}\n\\]",
